WEBVTT

00:00:16.720 --> 00:00:18.400
Speaker 1: So very many of us can relate to this.

00:00:18.400 --> 00:00:22.720
Speaker 1: Remember, Remember who your best friend was maybe when you were in primary school.

00:00:23.600 --> 00:00:32.430
Speaker 1: I'll never forget, there was this guy and I thought we really, I mean, cared about each other whilst we're in primary five.

00:00:32.830 --> 00:00:35.230
Speaker 1: Well, silly, or should I say naive me.

00:00:35.630 --> 00:00:45.470
Speaker 1: But when we got into secondary school, I never got the opportunity to see him until I think I was in JS3 or something, you know?

00:00:45.630 --> 00:00:52.190
Speaker 1: And so we happened to cross paths at the bookstore.

00:00:53.640 --> 00:00:54.480
Speaker 1: Just imagine this.

00:00:54.480 --> 00:01:00.200
Speaker 1: I was trying to buy something, and then he just steps in there ordering something.

00:01:00.760 --> 00:01:10.520
Speaker 1: So I call out his name, you know, in excitement, and then he looked at me and looked back and continued what he was ordering.

00:01:11.960 --> 00:01:17.480
Speaker 1: So at some point, at first I thought, oh, no, I'm sure he doesn't remember me.

00:01:17.880 --> 00:01:21.560
Speaker 1: But then, few minutes later, he looked at me, called my name and said, how are you doing?

00:01:21.560 --> 00:01:22.440
Speaker 1: I said, I'm fine.

00:01:22.610 --> 00:01:26.610
Speaker 1: And then he looked back and that was it, you know?

00:01:27.970 --> 00:01:38.210
Speaker 1: You know, so you see, some people think it's cool to see someone they used to be close to and pretend they didn't know the person.

00:01:38.770 --> 00:01:42.850
Speaker 1: And for some of us, maybe something legitimate happened.

00:01:42.850 --> 00:01:47.650
Speaker 1: Maybe you fell out, you got into a fight, even if it's verbal, all right?

00:01:47.650 --> 00:01:52.170
Speaker 1: And at first, for many people, this is what causes the heartbreak.

00:01:52.650 --> 00:01:55.210
Speaker 1: You tell this person, this is what you did.

00:01:56.810 --> 00:02:01.610
Speaker 1: And if the person just acknowledges, apologizes, you move on and you live happily ever after.

00:02:01.610 --> 00:02:09.210
Speaker 1: But then the person maybe doubles down or refuses to take accountability, and so you are hurt, terribly hurt, okay?

00:02:09.610 --> 00:02:16.270
Speaker 1: So for many people, you now say to yourself, I'll never talk to this person again, or we can't be close anymore, okay?

00:02:16.350 --> 00:02:20.110
Speaker 1: And that's how good relationships go south.

00:02:20.270 --> 00:02:26.990
Speaker 1: Now, believe it or not, in some way, shape or form, this is the Bible's narrative, all right?

00:02:27.550 --> 00:02:35.550
Speaker 1: The Bible starts with a beautiful relationship that God had with mankind, you know, in the Garden of Eden.

00:02:35.870 --> 00:02:40.430
Speaker 1: And then God said, hey, don't do this.

00:02:40.430 --> 00:02:47.960
Speaker 1: And, well, long story short, Adam did it and had to be driven out of the Garden of Eden, okay?

00:02:49.800 --> 00:03:03.160
Speaker 1: And so when we're talking about reconciliation, we're actually talking about the work of God through Christ, in which he removes hostility that was between himself and humanity, okay?

00:03:03.720 --> 00:03:12.150
Speaker 1: It's a very powerful story on relationship, divine relationship between God and us.

00:03:13.990 --> 00:03:24.870
Speaker 1: Some texts that I want to read, Romans 5, 10, 11, it says, for if while were enemies, were reconciled to God by the death of his son.

00:03:24.870 --> 00:03:25.670
Speaker 1: Much more.

00:03:26.070 --> 00:03:26.710
Speaker 1: All right?

00:03:27.510 --> 00:03:28.950
Speaker 1: We shall be saved by his life.

00:03:28.950 --> 00:03:29.550
Speaker 1: Much more.

00:03:29.550 --> 00:03:30.150
Speaker 1: Okay?

00:03:30.390 --> 00:03:32.150
Speaker 1: It says, were Enemies.

00:03:32.390 --> 00:03:34.150
Speaker 1: And then were reconciled.

00:03:34.310 --> 00:03:43.040
Speaker 1: So just in case you haven't noticed, over the past few days I've been getting you familiar with terminologies in soteriology.

00:03:43.040 --> 00:03:47.120
Speaker 1: Soteriology simply fancy theological term for the study of salvation.

00:03:47.600 --> 00:03:48.960
Speaker 1: So what are the terminologies?

00:03:48.960 --> 00:03:49.720
Speaker 1: You should know.

00:03:49.720 --> 00:03:54.800
Speaker 1: Propitiation, justification, redemption, and today reconciliation.

00:03:55.120 --> 00:03:58.320
Speaker 1: All right, were enemies and were reconciled.

00:03:58.320 --> 00:03:58.880
Speaker 1: Okay?

00:03:59.760 --> 00:04:09.800
Speaker 1: The Bible says in Second Corinthians 5, 18 and 19, you know, verse 7:17, if any man being Christ, it's a new creature, all things are passed away.

00:04:09.800 --> 00:04:11.160
Speaker 1: Behold, all things have become new.

00:04:11.160 --> 00:04:17.600
Speaker 1: And then it says all things of God who has reconciled us to himself by Jesus Christ.

00:04:17.920 --> 00:04:25.360
Speaker 1: All right, so there was hostility, but that hostility has been removed by what Christ did.

00:04:25.600 --> 00:04:37.930
Speaker 1: Just imagine, maybe just then when you were not talking to your friend, then a middleman comes and sits both of you down and says, you can't do this.

00:04:37.930 --> 00:04:38.250
Speaker 1: Okay?

00:04:38.250 --> 00:04:40.410
Speaker 1: And let's resolve this once and for all.

00:04:40.970 --> 00:04:48.170
Speaker 1: But in this context, it was God himself who, you know, came and did this.

00:04:48.330 --> 00:04:49.570
Speaker 1: He took the initiative.

00:04:49.570 --> 00:04:50.570
Speaker 1: We're going to go to that.

00:04:51.690 --> 00:04:55.530
Speaker 1: Now, what does the Old Testament say about reconciliation?

00:04:55.610 --> 00:05:02.070
Speaker 1: So in the Old Testament, a word, a Hebrew word that was used for reconciliation is kapha.

00:05:02.070 --> 00:05:04.750
Speaker 1: Now, now this is mind blowing, all right?

00:05:04.750 --> 00:05:28.120
Speaker 1: And those of you who have been following me have heard me teach this before because I remember sitting in my building technology class as an undergraduate and were told about some materials or some, yeah, materials that are used in building construction that can make a building impervious to rain.

00:05:28.120 --> 00:05:28.760
Speaker 1: All right?

00:05:29.000 --> 00:05:31.640
Speaker 1: So imagine you had a flat roof, okay?

00:05:31.960 --> 00:05:35.480
Speaker 1: You can put bitumen or something of that sort on it.

00:05:35.480 --> 00:05:39.320
Speaker 1: Or why do they use bitumen or tar on the road?

00:05:39.400 --> 00:05:41.800
Speaker 1: Is because it makes it impervious to rain.

00:05:41.800 --> 00:05:42.440
Speaker 1: Okay?

00:05:43.960 --> 00:06:00.620
Speaker 1: Now imagine my shock when, after class, was it before or after class, I was reading about the story of Noah, and God specifically told Noah to cover the ark with kapha so that rain will not be able to seep into it.

00:06:00.780 --> 00:06:01.420
Speaker 1: Okay?

00:06:02.300 --> 00:06:07.500
Speaker 1: If the ark of Noah was not impervious to rain, it would have still drowned.

00:06:08.140 --> 00:06:09.420
Speaker 1: And it's just brilliant.

00:06:09.660 --> 00:06:19.260
Speaker 1: Think about the level of building science that was available in those days by the wisdom of God conversation for another day.

00:06:20.150 --> 00:06:33.750
Speaker 1: So imagine my shock when I'm studying later on atonement, and I discover that the same word kapha was used, all right, to cover Leviticus 16:30.

00:06:34.150 --> 00:06:35.749
Speaker 1: To atone means to cover.

00:06:36.390 --> 00:06:37.030
Speaker 1: Okay?

00:06:37.670 --> 00:06:40.630
Speaker 1: I will tell you why this is important to cover.

00:06:41.830 --> 00:06:50.580
Speaker 1: So just the same way there was a covering on the, on Noah's ark to prevent the rain from coming, so falling in so that the.

00:06:50.580 --> 00:06:52.020
Speaker 1: The ark will not sink.

00:06:52.580 --> 00:06:54.900
Speaker 1: The blood of Jesus covers us.

00:06:55.300 --> 00:06:56.580
Speaker 1: That's our atonement.

00:06:56.900 --> 00:06:59.460
Speaker 1: And it is by that blood that we have reconciliation.

00:06:59.460 --> 00:07:02.100
Speaker 1: This is so powerful.

00:07:03.380 --> 00:07:12.340
Speaker 1: That is why our ship of salvation will not sink because we are covered, all right, by the blood, okay?

00:07:13.220 --> 00:07:16.470
Speaker 1: And that's what Paul was describing.

00:07:16.470 --> 00:07:22.550
Speaker 1: He has reconciled us to himself by Jesus Christ and given us the ministry of reconciliation, okay?

00:07:22.550 --> 00:07:27.750
Speaker 1: So that reconciliation came by the sacrifice of atonement of Jesus.

00:07:27.830 --> 00:07:42.790
Speaker 1: In the New Testament, the Greek word is katalasso, which kind of gives you a better idea of what I'm talking about, because katalasso means to exchange or, you know, so, yeah, it means to reconcile, but it also means to exchange.

00:07:43.190 --> 00:07:49.160
Speaker 1: So the reconciliation came by somebody standing in your place and dying your death, okay?

00:07:49.800 --> 00:07:57.880
Speaker 1: And so all in all, reconciliation was needed because there was hostility between God and mankind, all right?

00:07:58.440 --> 00:08:04.440
Speaker 1: And where there was to be canally mind that the Bible says is enmity against God.

00:08:04.440 --> 00:08:05.080
Speaker 1: Okay?

00:08:05.800 --> 00:08:10.360
Speaker 1: And then the agent or the means of reconciliation was Christ.

00:08:11.180 --> 00:08:13.860
Speaker 1: And it's important that I must re.

00:08:13.860 --> 00:08:15.260
Speaker 1: Emphasize Christ.

00:08:16.220 --> 00:08:19.260
Speaker 1: God was the initiator of this reconciliation.

00:08:19.260 --> 00:08:26.700
Speaker 1: I like the song that says, you did not wait for me to cry out to you before you clothe yourself in frail humanity.

00:08:26.700 --> 00:08:28.060
Speaker 1: That's describing the Incarnation.

00:08:28.140 --> 00:08:35.419
Speaker 1: You do not wait for me to call out to you before I heard your voice calling me.

00:08:35.500 --> 00:08:36.059
Speaker 1: All right?

00:08:36.220 --> 00:08:40.850
Speaker 1: So I think it's beautiful that at the moment you realized you were a sinner, you needed salvation.

00:08:41.330 --> 00:08:43.090
Speaker 1: The provision was already available.

00:08:43.970 --> 00:08:45.810
Speaker 1: God was the initiator.

00:08:46.130 --> 00:08:51.010
Speaker 1: As soon as man fell, he began to plan, you know, for the salvation.

00:08:51.010 --> 00:08:53.650
Speaker 1: In fact, he had planned it through his foreknowledge.

00:08:54.130 --> 00:08:56.930
Speaker 1: Paul said, before the foundation of the world, all right.

00:08:56.930 --> 00:09:00.810
Speaker 1: But as soon as man fell, he said, the seed of the woman who bruised the head of the serpent.

00:09:00.810 --> 00:09:06.540
Speaker 1: And I've explained how that was talking about how redemption will come.

00:09:07.020 --> 00:09:08.700
Speaker 1: So God is the initiator.

00:09:08.940 --> 00:09:10.860
Speaker 1: 2Nd Corinthians 5:18.

00:09:11.820 --> 00:09:13.740
Speaker 1: Christ's death is the means.

00:09:14.300 --> 00:09:16.700
Speaker 1: And then the Holy Spirit is the applier.

00:09:17.580 --> 00:09:19.820
Speaker 1: The Holy Spirit is the applier.

00:09:20.140 --> 00:09:23.260
Speaker 1: Romans, chapter 8, 15 and 16.

00:09:23.420 --> 00:09:30.410
Speaker 1: For you did not receive the spirit of bondage again to fear but the spirit of adoption, whereby we cry, abba, Father.

00:09:30.490 --> 00:09:31.090
Speaker 1: All right.

00:09:31.090 --> 00:09:34.650
Speaker 1: The Spirit bears witness with our spirits that we are children of God.

00:09:34.650 --> 00:09:38.810
Speaker 1: So when we receive the Holy Spirit, we have received the spirit of adoption.

00:09:38.810 --> 00:09:40.410
Speaker 1: That's our seal of adoption.

00:09:40.570 --> 00:09:44.690
Speaker 1: And we can cry abba, Father, because we are reconciled.

00:09:44.690 --> 00:09:45.770
Speaker 1: He's our daddy.

00:09:46.170 --> 00:09:46.730
Speaker 1: Forever.

00:09:47.210 --> 00:09:48.090
Speaker 1: Hallelujah.

00:09:48.410 --> 00:09:56.970
Speaker 1: If you are excited that you have been reconciled to him by the blood of Christ, I want you to just type thank you, Jesus in the comments.

00:09:57.600 --> 00:09:57.960
Speaker 1: All right?

00:09:57.960 --> 00:10:11.840
Speaker 1: And I want you to, just so that you carry the consciousness that this is not just theological discourse, I want you to thank him today for what he did through His Son.

00:10:12.400 --> 00:10:13.759
Speaker 1: Thank him for what he did.

00:10:14.000 --> 00:10:15.840
Speaker 1: One more text before I call it a day.

00:10:18.480 --> 00:10:22.560
Speaker 1: Ephesians, chapter 2, from verse 11.

00:10:23.400 --> 00:10:41.480
Speaker 1: It says, Therefore, remember that once you were Gentiles in the flesh who are called on circumcision by that which is called circumcision in the flesh, made by hands, that at that time you were without Christ, aliens from the commonwealth of Israel and strangers from the covenant of promise, having no hope and without God in the world.

00:10:41.640 --> 00:10:49.240
Speaker 1: But now in Christ, you who sometimes were afar off, have been brought nigh by the blood of Christ.

00:10:49.480 --> 00:10:51.240
Speaker 1: The blood of Christ has brought you near.

00:10:53.010 --> 00:10:54.770
Speaker 1: That's our reconciliation by blood.

00:10:55.170 --> 00:11:00.530
Speaker 1: He died just so that you can be brought into fellowship with God.

00:11:00.930 --> 00:11:03.570
Speaker 1: Thank you, Jesus, for what you've done.
